lina: Hey team — running into a weird one in production. Our rerank results suddenly have scores as booleans (true/false) instead of floats, so our sort is broken and UX shows wrong top result. Using TS SDK v0.8.1. Sample of what we send and what we receive below. Any ideas? :confused:

lina: Request (TS SDK call):
```ts
await client.rerank({
  model: 'redwood/rerank-v1',
  inputs: [
    { id: 'c1', text: 'candidate A', score: 1.0 },
    { id: 'c2', text: 'candidate B', score: 0.4 }
  ]
})
```

lina: Network response body we logged (server side):
```json
{
  "candidates": [
    { "id": "c1", "text": "candidate A", "score": true },
    { "id": "c2", "text": "candidate B", "score": false }
  ]
}
```

sofia: Thanks Lina — that definitely looks wrong. Rerank expects numeric scores (float). Couple quick Qs: 1) SDK version? 2) Any custom replacer/serializer on your side? 3) Is this intermittent or all requests?

lina: SDK is @redwood/sdk-js v0.8.1, no custom replacer. Happening for ~10% of requests in production, mostly high-throughput endpoints. No manual post-processing of the payload. We also saw increased p95 latency for rerank when this started.

mike: @lina can you paste your package.json deps and the exact TS call (copy-paste) you run in prod? Also any proxy/edge that might re-marshal JSON? I suspect a client-side serialization quirk under load.

lina: package.json deps snippet: 
```json
{ "dependencies": { "@redwood/sdk-js": "0.8.1", "node": "18.x" } }
```
Call is the same as above, running from edge worker that aggregates candidates. We log the outgoing body before the sdk call and it shows numbers; the incoming response (from Redwood) is where we see booleans.

aj: Quick thought — some libs use a custom replacer to drop nulls and might coerce truthy numbers with `!!` by mistake. Could be in SDK or infra layer. @mike any recent changes to serialization?

mike: Investigated locally with a minimal repro. Found a bug in the SDK's internal cleanup function used to strip undefined fields: for performance it does `const val = !!obj[k] ? obj[k] : undefined` which turns numeric 1 into `true`. Under certain candidate shapes the code path runs and mutates `score` to boolean before sending. Repro script below: 
```js
// repro.js
const cleanup = (obj) => {
  for (const k in obj) {
    // buggy: coerces numeric 1 -> true
    obj[k] = !!obj[k] ? obj[k] : undefined;
  }
  return obj;
}
const input = { id: 'c1', score: 1 };
console.log(cleanup(input)); // { id: 'c1', score: true }
```

mike: That matches your observation. This only triggers when score is exactly 1 and when the cleanup pass runs (which happens on high-concurrency path to dedupe empty fields). We're prepping a patch to avoid coercion, will push 0.8.2 today. Temporary workaround: coerce scores to non-1 floats (e.g., 1.0 -> 1.0001) or set explicit Number() when building the payload: `score: Number(candidate.score)` before calling SDK.

lina: Thanks — workaround applied and verified in staging, fixed ordering. Can you confirm ETA for patch? Also the latency bump we saw was weirdly correlated; could the cleanup cause extra CPU?

mike: Patch PR opened, targeting release in next ~2 hours. The cleanup did extra property walking under heavy load which raised p95 CPU and blocked the fast path briefly; fixing avoids the unnecessary walk for numeric fields. We'll post the release notes and link to the PR.

sofia: I'll create an internal ticket and mark this P2 with repro steps + workaround so CS teams can advise customers. @ci-bot can you open a ticket and add label `rerank-sdk-bug`?

ci-bot: Created support ticket #SUP-8942, label `rerank-sdk-bug`, assigned to mike. :white_check_mark:

mike: Thanks — will follow up with the release artifact and changelog. If anyone on the thread still sees boolean scores after 0.8.2, ping and I'll escalate to eng-runtime. :bug: :eyes:
